        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_27.lnote.oe.4-2" n="4" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">67</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">parte <hi rend="italic">fügt zu Brti</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">parte added by Brti</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_27.lnote.oe.5-3" n="5" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">67</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-ne <hi rend="italic">R Lond</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-ne R Lond</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_27.lnote.oe.6-3" n="6" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">67</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">ipsam <hi rend="italic">Lond</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">ipsam Lond</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_27.lnote.oe.7-3" n="7" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">67</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">ipsam <hi rend="italic">T;</hi> et <hi rend="italic">Brti</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">ipsam T; et Brti</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_27.lnote.oe.8-2" n="8" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">67</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">verbessert aus</hi> confugiat <hi rend="italic">K</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">corrected from confugiat K</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_27.lnote.oe.2-2" n="2" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">67</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">æ <hi rend="italic">neu über erstem</hi> a</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">æ newly written above the first a</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_27.lnote.oe.3-3" n="3" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">67</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">he <hi rend="italic">im</hi> 16. <hi rend="italic">Jh. eingefügt</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">he inserted in the 16th cent.</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_27.lnote.oe.4-3" n="4" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">67</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">keine Lücke Hs; ergänze jedoch aus H;</hi> ⁊ <hi rend="italic">im</hi> 16. <hi rend="italic">Jh. eingefügt</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">no gap in MS; supply, however, from H; ⁊ inserted in the 16th cent.</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_27.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">57</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> Sippe 8. 26a; Muttersippe 1</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> Sippe 8. 26a; Muttersippe 1</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_27.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">57</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Q <hi rend="smallcaps">i</hi> lautet Af am nächsten. Ebenso Hn 75, 10</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Q <hi rend="smallcaps">i</hi> is closest in wording to Af. Likewise Hn 75, 10</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_27.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">57</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> Genossenschaft 3ff. Lies in Übs. statt ‘für’ deutlicher: ‘anstelle von’; ebenso 27, 1 letzte Zeile</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> Genossenschaft 3ff. In the translation read, more clearly, ‘anstelle von’ instead of ‘für’; likewise 27, 1 last line</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_27.lnote.de.4" n="4" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">57</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> Wergeld 13. 15; friedlos 2b. 9d. 10e. Auch nachdem sich das Geschlecht Fehdelast abkaufte, trägt sie der Totschläger für den unbezahlten Rest des Wergeldes. Der Zusatz in Hn 75, 10 <hi rend="italic">uel componat</hi> konnte, selbst nachdem die verletzte Sippe auf Rache verzichtete, nur praktisch werden, wenn Totschläger genügend Vermögen besass. Offenbar aber für seine Zahlungsunfähigkeit ergeht Af’s Gesetz</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> Wergeld 13. 15; friedlos 2b. 9d. 10e. Even after the kindred has bought itself free of the burden of feud, the slayer bears it for the unpaid remainder of the wergild. The addition <hi rend="italic">uel componat</hi> in Hn 75, 10 could, even after the injured kindred had renounced vengeance, have practical effect only if the slayer possessed sufficient property. Af’s law, however, is evidently issued for the case of his inability to pay</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Af_27.attnote.fn.16" n="16" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">76</biblScope></bibl><label>mægleas</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">mæigleas</hi>. B.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Af_27.attnote.fn.17" n="17" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">76</biblScope></bibl><label>ófslea</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">on gefeohte mon of</hi>. So.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Af_27.attnote.fn.18" n="18" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">76</biblScope></bibl><label>gif</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">gif he</hi>. H.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Af_27.attnote.fn.19" n="19" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">76</biblScope></bibl><label>ða</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">hio</hi>. So.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Af_27.attnote.fn.20" n="20" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">76</biblScope></bibl><label>dæl</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">H.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="Af_27.attnote.n.1" n="1" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">196</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">In Ine, cap. 16 and 21, <hi rend="italic">gegildan</hi> seems to denote those who are associated with the defendant for purposes of payment, and the presumption is that relatives are meant. Here it would seem that persons who had no relatives, or relatives only on one side (in general, presumably manumitted slaves or their sons), had other such persons associated with them, in place of relatives, for the same purpose. Liebermann (see his <hi rend="italic">Glossar</hi>, <hi rend="italic">s.v.</hi> Genossenschaft, 3 ff.) takes a different view.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="Af_27.attnote.n.2" n="2" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">196</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Lit. ‘shall flee.’ B. and T. and Liebermann translate <hi rend="italic">he fleo</hi> as ‘he shall go into banishment.’ But do not the words rather mean, that in default of payment, the relatives of the dead man shall have the right of taking vengeance; cf. Ine, cap. 74 § 1.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="translationFootnote" xml:id="Af_27.whinote.n.3" n="3" source="#whitelock"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">414</biblScope></bibl><label>associates</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">on the <hi rend="italic">gegildan, see</hi> p. 400, n. 5. Here, at any rate, persons who are not kinsmen are meant.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="translationFootnote" xml:id="Af_27.whinote.n.4" n="4" source="#whitelock"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">414</biblScope></bibl><label>part]</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">From Nowell’s transcript and <hi rend="italic">Textus Roffensis,</hi> since it has been omitted in the oldest MS.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="translationFootnote" xml:id="Af_27.whinote.n.5" n="5" source="#whitelock"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">414</biblScope></bibl><label>flee</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">This shows that the payment of their proper share frees the kinsmen from the dangers of a vendetta, even if the whole wergild is not paid. The slayer himself remains exposed if his own third is unpaid.</seg></note>
      </div>
